<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khadija bint Khuwaylid</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 554–619</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Khadija al-Kubra, Mother of the Believers</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khadija bint Khuwaylid was the first wife of the Islamic prophet Muhammad and a wealthy merchant of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>. She employed Muhammad to manage a trade caravan to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and then offered him marriage; sources differ on her age at the time, giving about 28 or 40. Traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> accounts hold that she was the first person to accept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> after his first revelation. Sources disagree on how many children they had, most naming six, and Sunni and some Shia views differ on her earlier marriages. She died in November 619, and the year is known as the Year of Sorrow.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khadija-bint-khuwaylid.jpg" alt="Khadija bint Khuwaylid" width="330" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: UsmanullahPK · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khadijah.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Khadija's mother, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatima">Fatima</a> bint Za'idah, who died in 575, belonged to the Amir ibn Luayy clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> and was a third cousin of Muhammad's mother, Amina. Her father, Khuwaylid ibn Asad, was a merchant and a leader. According to some accounts he died around 585 in the Sacrilegious War, but according to others he was still alive when Khadija married Muhammad in 595.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunnis</a> hold that she married Atiq ibn 'A'idh al-Makhzumi and then Malik ibn Nabash al-Tamimi before Muhammad. Some Shia sources hold that she was a virgin at her marriage to Muhammad, find it improbable that a woman of her standing married men of those tribes, and say the children attributed to her earlier marriages were those of her sister Hala.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Khadija was a very successful merchant. It is said that her caravan equalled those of all the other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> traders put together. She did not travel with her caravans but employed others for a commission. She is said to have fed and clothed the poor and to have neither believed in nor worshipped idols, which was unusual in pre-Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>.</p><p>She hired Muhammad, then in his early twenties, for a trade journey to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and sent her servant Maysarah with him. Maysarah reported the honourable way he had conducted business, and the profit was twice what she had expected. She then had a friend, Nafisa, ask whether he would consider marriage. After their uncles agreed, Muhammad moved into her house.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> account she was the first to accept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and she comforted Muhammad when he returned in terror from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabal-al-nour">cave of Hira</a>. She is said to have used her wealth to ransom Muslim slaves and feed the community. In 616 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> declared a trade boycott against the Hashim clan, and she helped to maintain the community until it was lifted in late 619 or early 620.</p><p>She died in November 619 and was buried in the Jannat al-Mu'alla cemetery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>. Muslims honour her as Mother of the Believers, and she is sometimes counted among the Four Ladies of Heaven, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatima">Fatima</a>, Asiya and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Mary, mother of Jesus</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Khadija bint Khuwaylid” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
